[{:db/id 17592186105907 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8951c125-ece2-55e4-a6b5-7a7f9b036e1d" "question-54f335de-9889-5585-819e-c5ae71a53628"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105908 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-65ad1da6-49b7-5494-8267-2eea67ec5389"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106309 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨3,5,0⟩$, $y=⟨3,2,0⟩$ and $z=⟨3,4,5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}|-5|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 0)-5(3\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 0)+0 \\\\  & =30-75 \\\\  & =-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|-45|=45.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186104002 :answer-field/correct 17592186104005 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-8951c125-ece2-55e4-a6b5-7a7f9b036e1d" :question/id #uuid "8951c125-ece2-55e4-a6b5-7a7f9b036e1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-280669" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨3,0,-2⟩$, $y=⟨1,2,4⟩$, and $z=⟨-5,1,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1)+0+(-2)(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =-6-22 \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-28|=28.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a6a81b1b-3ad2-556f-aea1-11eab91bed3e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6a81b1b-3ad2-556f-aea1-11eab91bed3e" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6a81b1b-3ad2-556f-aea1-11eab91bed3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-17336941-881c-5fb2-a109-5b4a5addcfec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17336941-881c-5fb2-a109-5b4a5addcfec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17336941-881c-5fb2-a109-5b4a5addcfec" :answer/id #uuid "17336941-881c-5fb2-a109-5b4a5addcfec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-54f335de-9889-5585-819e-c5ae71a53628" :question/id #uuid "54f335de-9889-5585-819e-c5ae71a53628" :question/math-academy-id "q-280714" :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $p=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $q=⟨0,5,1⟩$, and $r=⟨4,0,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(30)-2(0\\cdot 6-1\\cdot 4)+3(0\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =30+8-60 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-22|=22.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-752c7d24-ba0b-5853-99e8-14fd968c0d02"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-752c7d24-ba0b-5853-99e8-14fd968c0d02" :answer-field/id #uuid "752c7d24-ba0b-5853-99e8-14fd968c0d02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ef21b691-d97c-5047-bd55-f0d2f57c360d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef21b691-d97c-5047-bd55-f0d2f57c360d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef21b691-d97c-5047-bd55-f0d2f57c360d" :answer/id #uuid "ef21b691-d97c-5047-bd55-f0d2f57c360d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-65ad1da6-49b7-5494-8267-2eea67ec5389" :question/id #uuid "65ad1da6-49b7-5494-8267-2eea67ec5389" :question/math-academy-id "q-280747" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨2,k,-1⟩$, $b=⟨-3,4,5⟩$, and $c=⟨6,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & k & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & k & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 1-5\\cdot k)-k(-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 6)-1(-3\\cdot k-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =8-10k+33k+3k+24 \\\\  & =26k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 26k+32 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{16}{13}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aefbe563-901b-5005-9895-d7c05a2acfbf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aefbe563-901b-5005-9895-d7c05a2acfbf" :answer-field/id #uuid "aefbe563-901b-5005-9895-d7c05a2acfbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-eea5ea1b-1584-5bf8-9c56-60e082cc3b30" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eea5ea1b-1584-5bf8-9c56-60e082cc3b30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eea5ea1b-1584-5bf8-9c56-60e082cc3b30" :answer/id #uuid "eea5ea1b-1584-5bf8-9c56-60e082cc3b30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106095 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $p=⟨3,t,t⟩$, $q=⟨1,3,1⟩$, and $r=⟨1,1,3⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & t & t \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|-t\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+t\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)-t(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)+t(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =24-2t-2t \\\\  & =24-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 24-4t & =0 \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102446 :answer-field/correct 17592186102450 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106044 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(9,6,1),$ $B(9,7,2),$ $C(7,5,2),$ and $D(7,8,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(9i+7j+2k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(7i+5j+2k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =-2i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(7i+8j+3k)-(9i+6j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-((-2)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2-6 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-4|=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102150 :answer-field/correct 17592186102155 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119838 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-12b4f5c6-720f-5c2c-b1e8-295eb7dcb29c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120290 :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\{\\begin{aligned}x=5-2t \\\\ y=-1+4t \\\\ z=-3-5t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:2x-5y+3z-84=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5y+3z-84 & =0 \\\\ 2(5-2t)-5(-1+4t)+3(-3-5t) & =84 \\\\ 10-4t+5-20t-9-15t & =84 \\\\ -39t & =78 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-2$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=5-2(-2)=9 \\\\ y=-1+4(-2)=-9 \\\\ z=-3-5(-2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(9,-9,7).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-12b4f5c6-720f-5c2c-b1e8-295eb7dcb29c" :question/id #uuid "12b4f5c6-720f-5c2c-b1e8-295eb7dcb29c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73991" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2-2t \\\\ y=5-3t \\\\ z=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:3x-2y+6z=62.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y+6z & =62 \\\\ 3(-2-2t)-2(5-3t)+6(-1+2t) & =62 \\\\ -6-6t-10+6t-6+12t & =62 \\\\ 12t & =84 \\\\ t & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=6$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2-2(7)=-16 \\\\ y=5-3(7)=-16 \\\\ z=-1+2(7)=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(-16,-16,13).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15f90597-4c03-5ae4-b2c2-f42eae091087"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15f90597-4c03-5ae4-b2c2-f42eae091087" :answer-field/id #uuid "15f90597-4c03-5ae4-b2c2-f42eae091087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-20fc86f8-0368-54eb-a3e0-3cf6b0379332" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20fc86f8-0368-54eb-a3e0-3cf6b0379332" "answer-7944f3e7-3b31-5e5a-b5cb-a1562208eb42" "answer-2143e54b-67d5-5002-957a-3bf88c230ca4" "answer-a0a4f2b9-c6e0-5e67-8ab6-66d592472d45" "answer-257b548a-b52d-5b2d-8a2f-57d74b50d39c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20fc86f8-0368-54eb-a3e0-3cf6b0379332" :answer/id #uuid "20fc86f8-0368-54eb-a3e0-3cf6b0379332" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-16,-16,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7944f3e7-3b31-5e5a-b5cb-a1562208eb42" :answer/id #uuid "7944f3e7-3b31-5e5a-b5cb-a1562208eb42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(11,-13,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2143e54b-67d5-5002-957a-3bf88c230ca4" :answer/id #uuid "2143e54b-67d5-5002-957a-3bf88c230ca4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(24,-11,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0a4f2b9-c6e0-5e67-8ab6-66d592472d45" :answer/id #uuid "a0a4f2b9-c6e0-5e67-8ab6-66d592472d45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(26,12,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-257b548a-b52d-5b2d-8a2f-57d74b50d39c" :answer/id #uuid "257b548a-b52d-5b2d-8a2f-57d74b50d39c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No intersection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186342127 :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3-5t \\\\ y=2-3t \\\\ z=-2-6t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:5x-2y+4z-132=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y+4z-132 & =0 \\\\ 5(3-5t)-2(2-3t)+4(-2-6t) & =132 \\\\ 15-25t-4+6t-8-24t & =132 \\\\ -43t & =129 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3-5(-3)=18 \\\\ y=2-3(-3)=11 \\\\ z=-2-6(-3)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(18,11,16).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-77955475-06cd-58a4-a89c-cfc9a4ad253a" :activity/id #uuid "77955475-06cd-58a4-a89c-cfc9a4ad253a" :activity/title "The Intersection Between a Line and a Plane" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050148 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-a9c592be-dcfe-59b6-a857-234bdfcf6a00" "step-77b76b91-5e55-576f-a898-b871a06b8ed3" "step-3315e388-6b5a-5594-b5f3-c37c6118ed26" "step-0227058d-e862-5e31-b5c8-bea4849a37b5" "step-6a1f4ccb-dfb0-5de6-aea8-7109eb01b3fc"] :activity/first-step "step-a9c592be-dcfe-59b6-a857-234bdfcf6a00" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a9c592be-dcfe-59b6-a857-234bdfcf6a00" :step/id #uuid "a9c592be-dcfe-59b6-a857-234bdfcf6a00" :step/content 17592186120290 :step/next "step-77b76b91-5e55-576f-a898-b871a06b8ed3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-77b76b91-5e55-576f-a898-b871a06b8ed3" :step/id #uuid "77b76b91-5e55-576f-a898-b871a06b8ed3" :step/content "question-12b4f5c6-720f-5c2c-b1e8-295eb7dcb29c" :step/next "step-3315e388-6b5a-5594-b5f3-c37c6118ed26" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3315e388-6b5a-5594-b5f3-c37c6118ed26" :step/id #uuid "3315e388-6b5a-5594-b5f3-c37c6118ed26" :step/content 17592186342127 :step/next "step-0227058d-e862-5e31-b5c8-bea4849a37b5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0227058d-e862-5e31-b5c8-bea4849a37b5" :step/id #uuid "0227058d-e862-5e31-b5c8-bea4849a37b5" :step/content 17592186342129 :step/next "step-6a1f4ccb-dfb0-5de6-aea8-7109eb01b3fc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6a1f4ccb-dfb0-5de6-aea8-7109eb01b3fc" :step/id #uuid "6a1f4ccb-dfb0-5de6-aea8-7109eb01b3fc" :step/content 17592186475912 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-88299f24-4f45-5ffe-aaf6-eeca4cfa5124"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-88299f24-4f45-5ffe-aaf6-eeca4cfa5124" :question/id #uuid "88299f24-4f45-5ffe-aaf6-eeca4cfa5124" :question/math-academy-id "q-75089" :question/problem "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$,  ${p}_{3}$ and ${p}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $P.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{4}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{4}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-259644ac-e832-5d7b-b706-9bf284412c4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-259644ac-e832-5d7b-b706-9bf284412c4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "259644ac-e832-5d7b-b706-9bf284412c4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-26229f41-5686-5fd3-9b06-e60b8419a926" :answer-field/choices ["answer-26842599-1eb5-50e0-859b-db04ef0c0686" "answer-e01b0506-44a8-5c88-a48e-942f949bf680" "answer-73e827cb-d4df-51d5-a0c2-8e504741edcd" "answer-26229f41-5686-5fd3-9b06-e60b8419a926" "answer-857b74b9-1552-5629-a376-93f606672083"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26842599-1eb5-50e0-859b-db04ef0c0686" :answer/id #uuid "26842599-1eb5-50e0-859b-db04ef0c0686" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e01b0506-44a8-5c88-a48e-942f949bf680" :answer/id #uuid "e01b0506-44a8-5c88-a48e-942f949bf680" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73e827cb-d4df-51d5-a0c2-8e504741edcd" :answer/id #uuid "73e827cb-d4df-51d5-a0c2-8e504741edcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26229f41-5686-5fd3-9b06-e60b8419a926" :answer/id #uuid "26229f41-5686-5fd3-9b06-e60b8419a926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-857b74b9-1552-5629-a376-93f606672083" :answer/id #uuid "857b74b9-1552-5629-a376-93f606672083" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4e023a9e-c79a-53ca-9a76-a188bd7ab902" "question-eb27e567-0848-5034-b8d8-32b6b421d46e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4e023a9e-c79a-53ca-9a76-a188bd7ab902" :question/id #uuid "4e023a9e-c79a-53ca-9a76-a188bd7ab902" :question/math-academy-id "q-75951" :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-64759caf-77ed-5792-a395-f9662dbea5af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-64759caf-77ed-5792-a395-f9662dbea5af" :answer-field/id #uuid "64759caf-77ed-5792-a395-f9662dbea5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12117f66-7e89-5080-9c67-2baf0fc5322d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c340483-1504-5c29-8184-e2464a93b17c" "answer-7ecb7b58-6f98-517c-8a10-0c822f40ce2c" "answer-b17ad927-4efe-5a54-8fec-c33efe368201" "answer-12117f66-7e89-5080-9c67-2baf0fc5322d" "answer-b5f2c10c-efb5-5f13-bc42-613ef41e630b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c340483-1504-5c29-8184-e2464a93b17c" :answer/id #uuid "4c340483-1504-5c29-8184-e2464a93b17c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ecb7b58-6f98-517c-8a10-0c822f40ce2c" :answer/id #uuid "7ecb7b58-6f98-517c-8a10-0c822f40ce2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b17ad927-4efe-5a54-8fec-c33efe368201" :answer/id #uuid "b17ad927-4efe-5a54-8fec-c33efe368201" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12117f66-7e89-5080-9c67-2baf0fc5322d" :answer/id #uuid "12117f66-7e89-5080-9c67-2baf0fc5322d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5f2c10c-efb5-5f13-bc42-613ef41e630b" :answer/id #uuid "b5f2c10c-efb5-5f13-bc42-613ef41e630b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eb27e567-0848-5034-b8d8-32b6b421d46e" :question/id #uuid "eb27e567-0848-5034-b8d8-32b6b421d46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111504" :question/problem "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$, and ${h}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${h}_{2}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{3}{h}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-572b5c18-b30c-522b-af06-1c2750b42b52"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-572b5c18-b30c-522b-af06-1c2750b42b52" :answer-field/id #uuid "572b5c18-b30c-522b-af06-1c2750b42b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b4f26072-e997-52aa-9840-7944fc90bd14" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ee1b5e6-8510-51f6-b77d-9e89493cdd8c" "answer-33ca77f7-231e-5f3a-9af5-87ee3086ded9" "answer-65aa6abc-3795-555d-a118-02cf871c6f2f" "answer-369b1b63-2671-515f-a882-54df1b9bcd81" "answer-b4f26072-e997-52aa-9840-7944fc90bd14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ee1b5e6-8510-51f6-b77d-9e89493cdd8c" :answer/id #uuid "3ee1b5e6-8510-51f6-b77d-9e89493cdd8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33ca77f7-231e-5f3a-9af5-87ee3086ded9" :answer/id #uuid "33ca77f7-231e-5f3a-9af5-87ee3086ded9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65aa6abc-3795-555d-a118-02cf871c6f2f" :answer/id #uuid "65aa6abc-3795-555d-a118-02cf871c6f2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-369b1b63-2671-515f-a882-54df1b9bcd81" :answer/id #uuid "369b1b63-2671-515f-a882-54df1b9bcd81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4f26072-e997-52aa-9840-7944fc90bd14" :answer/id #uuid "b4f26072-e997-52aa-9840-7944fc90bd14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-774a94cd-8530-573f-80d6-592f51a33b36" :activity/id #uuid "774a94cd-8530-573f-80d6-592f51a33b36" :activity/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049009 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-c7e441a7-a721-5e10-85ac-7c952dabd92c" "step-0b8b441f-1ac7-578f-9670-a1e7fdce2cfe" "step-2285bbbb-2b94-5bc1-a8a2-c4f8628b9d46" "step-17548c6c-a0db-59ae-8691-b5c2e89ec05c" "step-57e1f077-3a25-541d-a79a-09e739748679"] :activity/first-step "step-c7e441a7-a721-5e10-85ac-7c952dabd92c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c7e441a7-a721-5e10-85ac-7c952dabd92c" :step/id #uuid "c7e441a7-a721-5e10-85ac-7c952dabd92c" :step/content "question-88299f24-4f45-5ffe-aaf6-eeca4cfa5124" :step/next "step-0b8b441f-1ac7-578f-9670-a1e7fdce2cfe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0b8b441f-1ac7-578f-9670-a1e7fdce2cfe" :step/id #uuid "0b8b441f-1ac7-578f-9670-a1e7fdce2cfe" :step/content "question-4e023a9e-c79a-53ca-9a76-a188bd7ab902" :step/next "step-2285bbbb-2b94-5bc1-a8a2-c4f8628b9d46" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2285bbbb-2b94-5bc1-a8a2-c4f8628b9d46" :step/id #uuid "2285bbbb-2b94-5bc1-a8a2-c4f8628b9d46" :step/content 17592186345151 :step/next "step-17548c6c-a0db-59ae-8691-b5c2e89ec05c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-17548c6c-a0db-59ae-8691-b5c2e89ec05c" :step/id #uuid "17548c6c-a0db-59ae-8691-b5c2e89ec05c" :step/content 17592186423621 :step/next "step-57e1f077-3a25-541d-a79a-09e739748679" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-57e1f077-3a25-541d-a79a-09e739748679" :step/id #uuid "57e1f077-3a25-541d-a79a-09e739748679" :step/content "question-eb27e567-0848-5034-b8d8-32b6b421d46e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186067924 :answer-field/choices 17592186067925] [:db/retract 17592186067924 :answer-field/choices 17592186067926] [:db/retract 17592186067924 :answer-field/choices 17592186067927] [:db/retract 17592186067924 :answer-field/choices 17592186067928] {:db/id 17592186068818 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-36e8c9dd-410a-532e-8d7d-3fbe3d803c48" "question-a6e11119-0478-59ad-91a4-e980ea6fc7de"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068437 :question/worked-solution "In general, the fractional exponent $x^{1/n}$ can also be written as the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$ In this particular case, $n=3,$ $x=2a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n(2a)^{1/3} = \\sqrt[3]{2a}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068819 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4de24564-b425-5826-93e9-0754fb5ac312" "question-9ae71e99-ce7d-5fcc-9476-baa39934b2a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068438 :question/worked-solution "In general, the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/n}.$ In this particular case, $n=4,$ $x=2y,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2y} = (2y)^{1/4}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068820 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-796bbc6b-6296-53e2-9ba1-772a42c3682e" "question-a2119555-75f3-5c12-a19c-6b58c2203637" "question-d404dee0-5ff3-56cb-8a05-5c052ee8e4f6" "question-ed145bb6-4544-5180-95f9-0e7fbc3058cf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068440 :question/worked-solution "Remember that, in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^{1/2}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx^{-1/2} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068821 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e42777e5-f467-5afd-b7c7-cd6c6a0a5f16" "question-5cb7fb47-9257-5913-83dc-1bd76a83bc17" "question-772700a0-3639-5650-9270-38c3442d0377"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068441 :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}}$ using power notation." :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}} = \\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(2y)^{1/3}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3\\phantom{|}]{2y}} = (2y)^{-1/3}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-36e8c9dd-410a-532e-8d7d-3fbe3d803c48" :question/id #uuid "36e8c9dd-410a-532e-8d7d-3fbe3d803c48" :question/math-academy-id "q-73412" :question/problem "${(7y)}^{1/4}=$" :question/worked-solution "In general, the fractional exponent ${x}^{1/n}$ can also be written as the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$ In this particular case, $n=4,$ $x=7y,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n{(7y)}^{1/4}=\\sqrt[4]{7y}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08856f44-329b-5847-914d-e6584ab3ef54"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08856f44-329b-5847-914d-e6584ab3ef54" :answer-field/id #uuid "08856f44-329b-5847-914d-e6584ab3ef54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec9db6dd-ed55-58aa-8ed8-e5b651e3fd7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90fa5c7d-a368-5ede-bcae-709d5da7d98f" "answer-1031089c-5449-50b9-8e55-6d01405d29d9" "answer-ec9db6dd-ed55-58aa-8ed8-e5b651e3fd7d" "answer-06832b2f-02b8-5c41-951c-ac166fee297f" "answer-7a1a7e3f-7a9c-5fac-97dd-d7d844933081"] :db/ensure [:answer-field