field/choices ["answer-a1825e4b-b468-5ebc-a30f-cc61a9d35d9f" "answer-45b276c5-0251-580a-8640-efe60cef5fae" "answer-fe334f65-ade3-58d1-a2d1-c0388a0e27ab" "answer-d6e10df0-3a4a-5d10-8790-2571f24def65" "answer-053123e5-3a18-570e-ae84-136e92cd32a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1825e4b-b468-5ebc-a30f-cc61a9d35d9f" :answer/id #uuid "a1825e4b-b468-5ebc-a30f-cc61a9d35d9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{9}^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45b276c5-0251-580a-8640-efe60cef5fae" :answer/id #uuid "45b276c5-0251-580a-8640-efe60cef5fae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{9}^{1/t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe334f65-ade3-58d1-a2d1-c0388a0e27ab" :answer/id #uuid "fe334f65-ade3-58d1-a2d1-c0388a0e27ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{-1/9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6e10df0-3a4a-5d10-8790-2571f24def65" :answer/id #uuid "d6e10df0-3a4a-5d10-8790-2571f24def65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{1/9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-053123e5-3a18-570e-ae84-136e92cd32a1" :answer/id #uuid "053123e5-3a18-570e-ae84-136e92cd32a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-772700a0-3639-5650-9270-38c3442d0377" :question/id #uuid "772700a0-3639-5650-9270-38c3442d0377" :question/math-academy-id "q-72817" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{9t}}$ using power notation." :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{9t}}=\\frac{1}{(9t)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(9t)^{1/3}}=(9t)^{-1/3}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{9t}}=(9t)^{-1/3}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a8ec1b12-9e43-55f5-ad98-b83c87549d06"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a8ec1b12-9e43-55f5-ad98-b83c87549d06" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8ec1b12-9e43-55f5-ad98-b83c87549d06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5e93d90-77be-5754-8140-af66d48ce2d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5e93d90-77be-5754-8140-af66d48ce2d0" "answer-07d9a016-b134-5db2-9710-01edb7ff247e" "answer-808dd18e-64d4-561c-b454-8971694f94b2" "answer-5ddbd237-88d2-5e4d-b015-1a8e95ffc0cb" "answer-5ad214ee-1f98-5232-90ee-b390824d9859"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5e93d90-77be-5754-8140-af66d48ce2d0" :answer/id #uuid "f5e93d90-77be-5754-8140-af66d48ce2d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(9t)^{-1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07d9a016-b134-5db2-9710-01edb7ff247e" :answer/id #uuid "07d9a016-b134-5db2-9710-01edb7ff247e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(9t)^{-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-808dd18e-64d4-561c-b454-8971694f94b2" :answer/id #uuid "808dd18e-64d4-561c-b454-8971694f94b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(t)^{-1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ddbd237-88d2-5e4d-b015-1a8e95ffc0cb" :answer/id #uuid "5ddbd237-88d2-5e4d-b015-1a8e95ffc0cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{-1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ad214ee-1f98-5232-90ee-b390824d9859" :answer/id #uuid "5ad214ee-1f98-5232-90ee-b390824d9859" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069937 :tutorial/content "A radical expression like $\\sqrt{{\\color{black}x}}$ can also be written using a fractional exponent as $x^{1/2}.$ They mean the same thing.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- a cube root such as $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}3\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{3}},$\n\n- a tenth root $\\sqrt[{\\color{blue}10\\!\\phantom{p}}]{x}$ is equivalent to $x^{1/\\color{blue}{10}},$\n\nand so on. In general, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068928 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-92ddc7e5-b6d3-5672-8ade-2787d57915e1" "question-a7e2dc04-d285-5815-98e3-c99d4176897c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-92ddc7e5-b6d3-5672-8ade-2787d57915e1" :question/id #uuid "92ddc7e5-b6d3-5672-8ade-2787d57915e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132014" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{-2t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}{{e}^{3t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-795c37b4-5f92-5e3b-aa83-2f9b29aa6f03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-795c37b4-5f92-5e3b-aa83-2f9b29aa6f03" :answer-field/id #uuid "795c37b4-5f92-5e3b-aa83-2f9b29aa6f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0421500e-2616-57c8-9da2-689ccd40356c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0421500e-2616-57c8-9da2-689ccd40356c" "answer-668782ba-0b98-5b7f-b25e-71892937ec4e" "answer-6e3f626c-c384-5ec1-b671-ad33ea606c0c" "answer-ab6d79a5-1689-5b95-8ee8-41606efa235f" "answer-7d137241-98f2-54be-bd88-0f9e32e0f2bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0421500e-2616-57c8-9da2-689ccd40356c" :answer/id #uuid "0421500e-2616-57c8-9da2-689ccd40356c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-668782ba-0b98-5b7f-b25e-71892937ec4e" :answer/id #uuid "668782ba-0b98-5b7f-b25e-71892937ec4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e3f626c-c384-5ec1-b671-ad33ea606c0c" :answer/id #uuid "6e3f626c-c384-5ec1-b671-ad33ea606c0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab6d79a5-1689-5b95-8ee8-41606efa235f" :answer/id #uuid "ab6d79a5-1689-5b95-8ee8-41606efa235f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d137241-98f2-54be-bd88-0f9e32e0f2bf" :answer/id #uuid "7d137241-98f2-54be-bd88-0f9e32e0f2bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7e2dc04-d285-5815-98e3-c99d4176897c" :question/id #uuid "a7e2dc04-d285-5815-98e3-c99d4176897c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132051" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨4{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =4\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =4⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =(4⟨2t,\\,1,\\,0⟩)\\times (8⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩) \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-32\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{(4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{64\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-32a2621c-52bf-54ef-b979-b5a1bacfe6d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32a2621c-52bf-54ef-b979-b5a1bacfe6d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "32a2621c-52bf-54ef-b979-b5a1bacfe6d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6528173f-e8b1-5a42-8e0f-015496528630" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6528173f-e8b1-5a42-8e0f-015496528630" "answer-f20d2290-e591-52af-92b9-bd4181fb6027" "answer-b6c19760-4ded-5e81-b3c9-2b434625b1c4" "answer-abbefdf6-1ec8-5f22-84de-6723b7e1d7b6" "answer-26d1ccff-7494-5f51-adcb-7caa359b5d22"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6528173f-e8b1-5a42-8e0f-015496528630" :answer/id #uuid "6528173f-e8b1-5a42-8e0f-015496528630" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f20d2290-e591-52af-92b9-bd4181fb6027" :answer/id #uuid "f20d2290-e591-52af-92b9-bd4181fb6027" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6c19760-4ded-5e81-b3c9-2b434625b1c4" :answer/id #uuid "b6c19760-4ded-5e81-b3c9-2b434625b1c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abbefdf6-1ec8-5f22-84de-6723b7e1d7b6" :answer/id #uuid "abbefdf6-1ec8-5f22-84de-6723b7e1d7b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26d1ccff-7494-5f51-adcb-7caa359b5d22" :answer/id #uuid "26d1ccff-7494-5f51-adcb-7caa359b5d22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f88d5adc-1895-5ddf-9de6-45e58f509300" :activity/id #uuid "f88d5adc-1895-5ddf-9de6-45e58f509300" :activity/title "Finding Curvature Using the Cross Product" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048648 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-723416d5-8258-5723-a84d-1b6f2871325d" "step-8e9f96eb-3033-5b55-bf1d-bd63b264150d" "step-0d33b287-b538-55b3-b3c7-cf0142e26701" "step-bdfbf0ab-5e71-5eab-a28e-ae335d919cc6" "step-ec4b6043-94ea-5557-8d3d-cc725d5c7999"] :activity/first-step "step-723416d5-8258-5723-a84d-1b6f2871325d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-723416d5-8258-5723-a84d-1b6f2871325d" :step/id #uuid "723416d5-8258-5723-a84d-1b6f2871325d" :step/content 17592186317438 :step/next "step-8e9f96eb-3033-5b55-bf1d-bd63b264150d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8e9f96eb-3033-5b55-bf1d-bd63b264150d" :step/id #uuid "8e9f96eb-3033-5b55-bf1d-bd63b264150d" :step/content 17592186368805 :step/next "step-0d33b287-b538-55b3-b3c7-cf0142e26701" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0d33b287-b538-55b3-b3c7-cf0142e26701" :step/id #uuid "0d33b287-b538-55b3-b3c7-cf0142e26701" :step/content 17592186354511 :step/next "step-bdfbf0ab-5e71-5eab-a28e-ae335d919cc6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bdfbf0ab-5e71-5eab-a28e-ae335d919cc6" :step/id #uuid "bdfbf0ab-5e71-5eab-a28e-ae335d919cc6" :step/content "question-92ddc7e5-b6d3-5672-8ade-2787d57915e1" :step/next "step-ec4b6043-94ea-5557-8d3d-cc725d5c7999" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ec4b6043-94ea-5557-8d3d-cc725d5c7999" :step/id #uuid "ec4b6043-94ea-5557-8d3d-cc725d5c7999" :step/content "question-a7e2dc04-d285-5815-98e3-c99d4176897c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064297 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a2c4c6dc-06db-5c59-88cb-4cad1074fe93"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2c4c6dc-06db-5c59-88cb-4cad1074fe93" :question/id #uuid "a2c4c6dc-06db-5c59-88cb-4cad1074fe93" :question/math-academy-id "q-143480" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{121}$ be a random sample of size $n=121$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =300$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =55.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $295.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=121,\\,\\mu =300,\\,\\sigma =55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(300,\\frac{{55}^{2}}{121}) \\\\  & ∼N(300,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<295).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =P(Z<\\frac{295-300}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1) \\\\  & =Φ(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1)=0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =Φ(-1) \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd48dcdf-ec5c-553b-93df-1ad0ce12e474"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd48dcdf-ec5c-553b-93df-1ad0ce12e474" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd48dcdf-ec5c-553b-93df-1ad0ce12e474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1f98fc2b-1e1b-5ec3-bfa8-3c6f3b84ee29" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1d40bd2c-dcd9-59ac-b0e2-c7d52d41a210" "answer-1f98fc2b-1e1b-5ec3-bfa8-3c6f3b84ee29" "answer-2d0c2446-935e-58d5-bb0b-b9f993444007" "answer-91359df8-d289-5d02-8538-3a34ade27caa" "answer-b80bbc72-56db-5e70-9204-c74bdefa7b84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d40bd2c-dcd9-59ac-b0e2-c7d52d41a210" :answer/id #uuid "1d40bd2c-dcd9-59ac-b0e2-c7d52d41a210" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1539" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f98fc2b-1e1b-5ec3-bfa8-3c6f3b84ee29" :answer/id #uuid "1f98fc2b-1e1b-5ec3-bfa8-3c6f3b84ee29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1587" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d0c2446-935e-58d5-bb0b-b9f993444007" :answer/id #uuid "2d0c2446-935e-58d5-bb0b-b9f993444007" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1814" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91359df8-d289-5d02-8538-3a34ade27caa" :answer/id #uuid "91359df8-d289-5d02-8538-3a34ade27caa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2061" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b80bbc72-56db-5e70-9204-c74bdefa7b84" :answer/id #uuid "b80bbc72-56db-5e70-9204-c74bdefa7b84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2119" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-95a41c31-9a32-5733-b64d-135e1d1b45d5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-95a41c31-9a32-5733-b64d-135e1d1b45d5" :question/id #uuid "95a41c31-9a32-5733-b64d-135e1d1b45d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143502" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66,$ calculate $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0606\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0594\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0582\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0735\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0721\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0708\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0885\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0853\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7088\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7123\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7157\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7422\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7454\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7486\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7764\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7794\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{45}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{128}{45})-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{45}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.2,\\frac{0.66}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.2,0.022).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =P(\\frac{2-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}<Z<\\frac{2.3-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.35<Z<0.67) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.67)-P(Z<-1.35) \\\\  & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.67)=0.7486, \\\\ Φ(-1.35)=0.0885.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35) \\\\  & =0.7486-0.0885 \\\\  & =0.6601.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa4e5556-e66f-5ee1-bddc-c98cca0920b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa4e5556-e66f-5ee1-bddc-c98cca0920b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4e5556-e66f-5ee1-bddc-c98cca0920b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer-field/choices ["answer-40ba174f-6f4c-59fc-b3cb-137e1297aa50" "answer-82de29c2-442b-50b3-b8a2-f2cef0536930" "answer-554630d6-0001-5d34-98b7-ae518e793543" "answer-da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" "answer-e46f85da-0699-50b2-9c52-f46103534b9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40ba174f-6f4c-59fc-b3cb-137e1297aa50" :answer/id #uuid "40ba174f-6f4c-59fc-b3cb-137e1297aa50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5429" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82de29c2-442b-50b3-b8a2-f2cef0536930" :answer/id #uuid "82de29c2-442b-50b3-b8a2-f2cef0536930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5678" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-554630d6-0001-5d34-98b7-ae518e793543" :answer/id #uuid "554630d6-0001-5d34-98b7-ae518e793543" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer/id #uuid "da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6601" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e46f85da-0699-50b2-9c52-f46103534b9a" :answer/id #uuid "e46f85da-0699-50b2-9c52-f46103534b9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7123" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064506 :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n=25$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.15}{25}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =P(Z>\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>1.29) & =1-P(Z<1.29) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.29)=0.9015.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =1-Φ(1.29) \\\\  & =1-0.9015 \\\\  & =0.0985.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061266 :answer-field/correct 17592186061267 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5574158c-e378-5c22-867d-7f3ecb6e0b26" "question-a7a75f10-4331-5d53-8955-dd0d91fe7458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5574158c-e378-5c22-867d-7f3ecb6e0b26" :question/id #uuid "5574158c-e378-5c22-867d-7f3ecb6e0b26" :question/math-academy-id "q-143513" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{116}$ be a random sample of size $n=116$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3$ and $5.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=116,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.2+15\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{15}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.5+20+22.5)-16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{29}{116}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5) & ≈P(\\frac{3-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{5-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-2<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5)=Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9247b964-7703-5bda-8b8b-02af0509ca08"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9247b964-7703-5bda-8b8b-02af0509ca08" :answer-field/id #uuid "9247b964-7703-5bda-8b8b-02af0509ca08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-900ec749-ed02-5243-a1b6-eea5f267a77c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ecf42980-f617-5f97-b30d-0955b76d372f" "answer-58e231ce-e2dc-5918-a3f7-c22726470c58" "answer-c02cc025-e442-50e2-9860-12b5a1a52c28" "answer-900ec749-ed02-5243-a1b6-eea5f267a77c" "answer-b9fe1f0e-03f6-5c1b-9efa-37601b693f09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecf42980-f617-5f97-b30d-0955b76d372f" :answer/id #uuid "ecf42980-f617-5f97-b30d-0955b76d372f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58e231ce-e2dc-5918-a3f7-c22726470c58" :answer/id #uuid "58e231ce-e2dc-5918-a3f7-c22726470c58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c02cc025-e442-50e2-9860-12b5a1a52c28" :answer/id #uuid "c02cc025-e442-50e2-9860-12b5a1a52c28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.5)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-900ec749-ed02-5243-a1b6-eea5f267a77c" :answer/id #uuid "900ec749-ed02-5243-a1b6-eea5f267a77c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9fe1f0e-03f6-5c1b-9efa-37601b693f09" :answer/id #uuid "b9fe1f0e-03f6-5c1b-9efa-37601b693f09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(2.5)-Φ(-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7a75f10-4331-5d53-8955-dd0d91fe7458" :question/id #uuid "a7a75f10-4331-5d53-8955-dd0d91fe7458" :question/math-academy-id "q-143514" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{65}$ be a random sample of size $n=65$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $-4.9.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=65,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.1+(-6)\\cdot 0.4+(-4)\\cdot 0.4+(-2)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.8-2.4-1.6-0.2 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-6)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(6.4+14.4+6.4+0.4)-25 \\\\  & =2.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{2.6}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-4.9-(-5)}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9)=Φ(0.5).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3fbc75be-8e9b-55b7-b598-a4421edd592d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3fbc75be-8e9b-55b7-b598-a4421edd592d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbc75be-8e9b-55b7-b598-a4421edd592d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d30411eb-c7c7-50c0-be48-8d31e7a05041" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a9ebc6d-26da-5439-8344-0e789ccaddc9" "answer-2218abf4-e023-5af3-b0eb-30e08e6ee534" "answer-c7238fb6-2845-5bcb-8fb3-76e79b105b2e" "answer-d30411eb-c7c7-50c0-be48-8d31e7a05041" "answer-1e0e9d16-85d3-5733-86dd-b370bb650371"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a9ebc6d-26da-5439-8344-0e789ccaddc9" :answer/id #uuid "7a9ebc6d-26da-5439-8344-0e789ccaddc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(0.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2218abf4-e023-5af3-b0eb-30e08e6ee534" :answer/id #uuid "2218abf4-e023-5af3-b0eb-30e08e6ee534" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7238fb6-2845-5bc