 ["question-cd425a39-318f-5566-a2e2-b27e6f8eab87" "question-34386db1-c26a-52c0-8482-81ad51998468"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd425a39-318f-5566-a2e2-b27e6f8eab87" :question/id #uuid "cd425a39-318f-5566-a2e2-b27e6f8eab87" :question/math-academy-id "q-223098" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩.\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28d3585e-134e-53e5-b3ff-d54d2085a716"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28d3585e-134e-53e5-b3ff-d54d2085a716" :answer-field/id #uuid "28d3585e-134e-53e5-b3ff-d54d2085a716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac" :answer/id #uuid "9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186289935 :answer-field/correct "answer-099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer/id #uuid "099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-34386db1-c26a-52c0-8482-81ad51998468" :question/id #uuid "34386db1-c26a-52c0-8482-81ad51998468" :question/math-academy-id "q-358781" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-83efe503-c6c7-5398-8b5a-e3c31636860c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83efe503-c6c7-5398-8b5a-e3c31636860c" :answer-field/id #uuid "83efe503-c6c7-5398-8b5a-e3c31636860c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628" :answer/id #uuid "3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-de0bb7f6-cebe-5043-a82b-316bfc3bd5eb" :activity/id #uuid "de0bb7f6-cebe-5043-a82b-316bfc3bd5eb" :activity/title "Principal Normal Vectors" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048616 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-7532dca0-e492-58d7-9fd4-a9d9dfcd23c7" "step-51cd3660-dac6-5f65-9a77-3570d44fead1" "step-97701ad9-732a-5b50-b89b-ace9fb233738" "step-d0a76b8b-38c9-52ec-a2f0-64392234672c" "step-3dc5246d-6b1c-5f6e-8d4b-2f726b761e7e"] :activity/first-step "step-7532dca0-e492-58d7-9fd4-a9d9dfcd23c7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7532dca0-e492-58d7-9fd4-a9d9dfcd23c7" :step/id #uuid "7532dca0-e492-58d7-9fd4-a9d9dfcd23c7" :step/content 17592186345091 :step/next "step-51cd3660-dac6-5f65-9a77-3570d44fead1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-51cd3660-dac6-5f65-9a77-3570d44fead1" :step/id #uuid "51cd3660-dac6-5f65-9a77-3570d44fead1" :step/content 17592186398326 :step/next "step-97701ad9-732a-5b50-b89b-ace9fb233738" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-97701ad9-732a-5b50-b89b-ace9fb233738" :step/id #uuid "97701ad9-732a-5b50-b89b-ace9fb233738" :step/content "question-cd425a39-318f-5566-a2e2-b27e6f8eab87" :step/next "step-d0a76b8b-38c9-52ec-a2f0-64392234672c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d0a76b8b-38c9-52ec-a2f0-64392234672c" :step/id #uuid "d0a76b8b-38c9-52ec-a2f0-64392234672c" :step/content 17592186290399 :step/next "step-3dc5246d-6b1c-5f6e-8d4b-2f726b761e7e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3dc5246d-6b1c-5f6e-8d4b-2f726b761e7e" :step/id #uuid "3dc5246d-6b1c-5f6e-8d4b-2f726b761e7e" :step/content "question-34386db1-c26a-52c0-8482-81ad51998468" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186066498 :answer-field/choices 17592186066499] [:db/retract 17592186066498 :answer-field/choices 17592186066500] [:db/retract 17592186066498 :answer-field/choices 17592186066501] [:db/retract 17592186066498 :answer-field/choices 17592186066502] [:db/retract 17592186066498 :answer-field/choices 17592186066503] {:db/id 17592186068847 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fbd5a08f-884b-5546-b21c-f131de083119" "question-e0c969ed-b329-51a2-928a-6ee83a4be196"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068464 :question/problem "Factor $9x^2+30x+25.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 =  (a+{\\color{blue}{b}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9x^2+30x+25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}+30x+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}+2(15x)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(15x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}+2(3x\\cdot 5)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=3x$ and ${\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068902 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f1a4e854-18be-5044-a0a8-f098474cee63" "question-8c53c9a4-fe1b-5044-8911-e4ffad0659c2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068520 :question/problem "Factor $9y^2-12y+4.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 =  (a-{\\color{blue}{b}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9y^2-12y+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-12y+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-2(6y)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6y)$ as a product of $3y$ and ${\\color{blue}2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-2(3y\\cdot 2)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=3y$ and ${\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068848 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-388f80cd-cabc-537e-b7cd-20f266912f80"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068465 :question/problem "Factor the expression $7a^2+28a+28.$" :question/worked-solution "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $7.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}^{2}+28a+28 & = \\\\ 7({a}^{2}+4a+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+4a+4 & = \\\\ {a}^{2}+4a+{2}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 2a+{2}^{2} \\\\ (a+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({a}^{2}+4a+4) & = \\\\ 7(a+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068849 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e1247d09-04db-57b2-b09e-dc5dafe32a21" "question-66f132c4-a82c-5cf3-bd20-df4c38d0e1c3" "question-f2d099da-828c-5882-bf9e-a7fe2ace1301" "question-fa78e3d6-5bc5-5702-a92b-18645aa6667d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068466 :question/problem "Factor ![](images/a0/a026f57159735d92a77f9fe46f17720e60e27081143e9fa0e128a69ca1e56687.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out ![](images/66/66ef4b5796888b4025ee2d8b3d516ec80741b341db9389696b6e77a4af5208d8.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/19/1950433407a5924a7809709ff5239c25e9132f229b4aa157d0af383d1f08b30c.svg)\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n![](images/ba/baa2bc75c152c9a312cd1c09da0ac322782577892fc11679d8b5277a38ab627f.svg)\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a0fc84a06635785af990620d5d671329588a8018c0e0cdbc6beb130a23596070.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-fbd5a08f-884b-5546-b21c-f131de083119" :question/id #uuid "fbd5a08f-884b-5546-b21c-f131de083119" :question/math-academy-id "q-31972" :question/problem "$9{b}^{2}+24b+16=$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}=(x+y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{b}^{2}+24b+16.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b)^{2}+24b+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b)^{2}+2(12b)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(12b)$ as a product of $3b$ and $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b)^{2}+2(3b\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of a sum formula with $x=3b$ and $y=4.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b+4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a3075a11-4841-552d-9a9c-371f66cbeacf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a3075a11-4841-552d-9a9c-371f66cbeacf" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3075a11-4841-552d-9a9c-371f66cbeacf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-60fe1379-40b3-5b23-8fcb-7f7586ab9faf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60fe1379-40b3-5b23-8fcb-7f7586ab9faf" "answer-8c0d9f8a-93d7-5814-afab-17aea173f03a" "answer-e377712e-07aa-508a-8ac7-a9155b459a2f" "answer-4dcae35e-8c01-51f1-b995-5585f908d558" "answer-c3cd8909-6923-56a3-937b-12e62774254a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60fe1379-40b3-5b23-8fcb-7f7586ab9faf" :answer/id #uuid "60fe1379-40b3-5b23-8fcb-7f7586ab9faf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3b+4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c0d9f8a-93d7-5814-afab-17aea173f03a" :answer/id #uuid "8c0d9f8a-93d7-5814-afab-17aea173f03a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6b+4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e377712e-07aa-508a-8ac7-a9155b459a2f" :answer/id #uuid "e377712e-07aa-508a-8ac7-a9155b459a2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(b+4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dcae35e-8c01-51f1-b995-5585f908d558" :answer/id #uuid "4dcae35e-8c01-51f1-b995-5585f908d558" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(b+4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3cd8909-6923-56a3-937b-12e62774254a" :answer/id #uuid "c3cd8909-6923-56a3-937b-12e62774254a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9(b+4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0c969ed-b329-51a2-928a-6ee83a4be196" :question/id #uuid "e0c969ed-b329-51a2-928a-6ee83a4be196" :question/math-academy-id "q-111626" :question/problem "Factor  $9{p}^{2}+30p+25.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}=(a+b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{p}^{2}+30p+25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+30p+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+2(15p)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(15p)$ as a product of $3p$ and $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+2(3p\\cdot 5)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of a sum formula with $a=3p$ and $b=5.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb1c333a-4bd7-5452-8866-0435e2c0c3f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb1c333a-4bd7-5452-8866-0435e2c0c3f7" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1c333a-4bd7-5452-8866-0435e2c0c3f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9815f03d-6b3e-52d4-a70a-d0a2fc852f5e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88ce1007-7187-5a08-b20b-83ee756b7b51" "answer-9815f03d-6b3e-52d4-a70a-d0a2fc852f5e" "answer-536a6308-3a12-59f9-9d32-d8722745a586" "answer-3dd3d054-d811-5a53-9d2e-7f3072edd7b3" "answer-2d19409c-79ac-59fd-950f-09dd9f610f9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88ce1007-7187-5a08-b20b-83ee756b7b51" :answer/id #uuid "88ce1007-7187-5a08-b20b-83ee756b7b51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3p+25)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9815f03d-6b3e-52d4-a70a-d0a2fc852f5e" :answer/id #uuid "9815f03d-6b3e-52d4-a70a-d0a2fc852f5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3p+5)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-536a6308-3a12-59f9-9d32-d8722745a586" :answer/id #uuid "536a6308-3a12-59f9-9d32-d8722745a586" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3p+6)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dd3d054-d811-5a53-9d2e-7f3072edd7b3" :answer/id #uuid "3dd3d054-d811-5a53-9d2e-7f3072edd7b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(9p+25)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d19409c-79ac-59fd-950f-09dd9f610f9c" :answer/id #uuid "2d19409c-79ac-59fd-950f-09dd9f610f9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(9p+5)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1a4e854-18be-5044-a0a8-f098474cee63" :question/id #uuid "f1a4e854-18be-5044-a0a8-f098474cee63" :question/math-academy-id "q-78999" :question/problem "$36{x}^{2}-12x+1=$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n36{x}^{2}-12x+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x)^{2}-12x+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x)^{2}-2(6x)+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $6x$ and $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x)^{2}-2(6x\\cdot 1)+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=6x$ and $b=1.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f8ca3e2-cc88-59be-bfd9-57ec0a371ea3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f8ca3e2-cc88-59be-bfd9-57ec0a371ea3" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8ca3e2-cc88-59be-bfd9-57ec0a371ea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d8ac49d0-2736-55c5-9942-c5f4bc841709" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8ac49d0-2736-55c5-9942-c5f4bc841709" "answer-5cbe42d0-5a14-51ae-9aae-3f8f619c6b40" "answer-caa363bc-356f-5db2-a32a-2870062e1691" "answer-df7ef7d6-c82e-5e7e-a125-b9daa1e2b007" "answer-fb7ad599-6f0b-52c7-83e9-bd7c406bb117"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8ac49d0-2736-55c5-9942-c5f4bc841709" :answer/id #uuid "d8ac49d0-2736-55c5-9942-c5f4bc841709" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cbe42d0-5a14-51ae-9aae-3f8f619c6b40" :answer/id #uuid "5cbe42d0-5a14-51ae-9aae-3f8f619c6b40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caa363bc-356f-5db2-a32a-2870062e1691" :answer/id #uuid "caa363bc-356f-5db2-a32a-2870062e1691" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(3x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df7ef7d6-c82e-5e7e-a125-b9daa1e2b007" :answer/id #uuid "df7ef7d6-c82e-5e7e-a125-b9daa1e2b007" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(2x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb7ad599-6f0b-52c7-83e9-bd7c406bb117" :answer/id #uuid "fb7ad599-6f0b-52c7-83e9-bd7c406bb117" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6(x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c53c9a4-fe1b-5044-8911-e4ffad0659c2" :question/id #uuid "8c53c9a4-fe1b-5044-8911-e4ffad0659c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-44097" :question/problem "$25{x}^{2}-20x+4=$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n25{x}^{2}-20x+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-20x+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-2(10x)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(10x)$ as a product of $5x$ and $2:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-2(5x\\cdot 2)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=5x$ and $b=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-786f4e79-9f1f-565c-8ea8-e42cbee17fa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-786f4e79-9f1f-565c-8ea8-e42cbee17fa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "786f4e79-9f1f-565c-8ea8-e42cbee17fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-98c448ff-a0c7-5c15-a39d-60be4b862046" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bb4ba03-b841-5252-b936-59e69f24bc6f" "answer-98c448ff-a0c7-5c15-a39d-60be4b862046" "answer-f3f7001e-8b3a-512a-8dae-7003d254d43a" "answer-06854b84-638b-5f5d-98ab-d1f9249c195b" "answer-44eba7b4-09d5-519c-93dc-3c716aa811f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bb4ba03-b841-5252-b936-59e69f24bc6f" :answer/id #uuid "4bb4ba03-b841-5252-b936-59e69f24bc6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98c448ff-a0c7-5c15-a39d-60be4b862046" :answer/id #uuid "98c448ff-a0c7-5c15-a39d-60be4b862046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5x-2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3f7001e-8b3a-512a-8dae-7003d254d43a" :answer/id #uuid "f3f7001e-8b3a-512a-8dae-7003d254d43a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5x-3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06854b84-638b-5f5d-98ab-d1f9249c195b" :answer/id #uuid "06854b84-638b-5f5d-98ab-d1f9249c195b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(5x-4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44eba7b4-09d5-519c-93dc-3c716aa811f9" :answer/id #uuid "44eba7b4-09d5-519c-93dc-3c716aa811f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(5x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-388f80cd-cabc-537e-b7cd-20f266912f80" :question/id #uuid "388f80cd-cabc-537e-b7cd-20f266912f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-111645" :question/problem "Factor the expression $98{s}^{2}+84s+18.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98{s}^{2}+84s+18 & = \\\\ 2(49{s}^{2}+42s+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{s}^{2}+42s+9 & = \\\\ (7s)^{2}+42s+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (7s)^{2}+2\\cdot (7s\\cdot 3)+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (7s+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(49{s}^{2}+42s+9) & = \\\\ 2(7s+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5473ce96-587f-50c7-8c11-bf336478f1fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5473ce96-587f-50c7-8c11-bf336478f1fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "5473ce96-587f-50c7-8c11-bf336478f1fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d2c86ffd-4db0-580e-abe6-74afa5106fad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a4e64c9-9d89-58f4-b104-fef2a5ab53b2" "answer-1c3ae2ef-052d-59ef-8da9-57e36ce62f93" "answer-d2c86ffd-4db0-580e-abe6-74afa5106fad" "answer-872747f8-5478-5d1f-be17-16277c421081" "answer-c3f5b508-c6e2-5175-bc3f-7cda2a58573c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a4e64c9-9d89-58f4-b104-fef2a5ab53b2" :answer/id #uuid "4a4e64c9-9d89-58f4-b104-fef2a5ab53b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(2s+7)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c3ae2ef-052d-59ef-8da9-57e36ce62f93" :answer/id #uuid "1c3ae2ef-052d-59ef-8da9-57e36ce62f93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(3s+7)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2c86ffd-4db0-580e-abe6-74afa5106fad" :answer/id #uuid "d2c86ffd-4db0-580e-abe6-74afa5106fad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(7s+3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-872747f8-5478-5d1f-be17-16277c421081" :answer/id #uuid "872747f8-5478-5d1f-be17-16277c421081" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(7s+2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3f5b508-c6e2-5175-bc3f-7cda2a58573c" :answer/id #uuid "c3f5b508-c6e2-5175-bc3f-7cda2a58573c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(7s+3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069220 :question/problem "Factor $25{y}^{2}+50y+25.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $25.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $25,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}+50y+25 & = \\\\ 25({y}^{2}+2y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y+1 & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({y}^{2}+2y+1) & = \\\\ 25(y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066498 :answer-field/correct "answer-ca444773-3498-5cfe-aabc-eb03a3f76fea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-318d748e-c355-5a16-a7b0-f432ca157de2" "answer-ca444773-3498-5cfe-aabc-eb03a3f76fea" "answer-12aaa2df-cbfc-5d4a-8f1a-02ca44658d0f" "answer-94ba7ec0-9791-572d-ba76-db6608a2e991" "answer-55a4d5a2-d6d5-5c01-98a8-f81176186f00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-318d748e-c355-5a16-a7b0-f432ca157de2" :answer/id #uuid "318d748e-c355-5a16-a7b0-f432ca157de2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25(5y+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca444773-3498-5cfe-aabc-eb03a3f76fea" :answer/id #uuid "ca444773-3498-5cfe-aabc-eb03a3f76fea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25(y+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12aaa2df-cbfc-5d4a-8f1a-02ca44658d0f" :answer/id #uuid "12aaa2df-cbfc-5d4a-8f1a-02ca44658d0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25({y}^{2}+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94ba7ec0-9791-572d-ba76-db6608a2e991" :answer/id #uuid "94ba7ec0-9791-572d-ba76-db6608a2e991" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(5y+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55a4d5a2-d6d5-5c01-98a8-f81176186f00" :answer/id #uuid "55a4d5a2-d6d5-5c01-98a8-f81176186f00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(5{y}^{2}+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1247d09-04db-57b2-b09e-dc5dafe32a21" :question/id #uuid "e1247d09-04db-57b2-b09e-dc5dafe32a21" :question/math-academy-id "q-111652" :question/problem "Factor $-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9} & = \\\\ -(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9} & = \\\\ (4k)^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ (4k)^{2}-2\\cdot (4k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9}) & = \\\\ -{(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89b397e2-7683-5850-8d9c-30d28cd8ea87"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89b397e2-7683-5850-8d9c-30d28cd8ea87" :answer-field/id #uuid "89b397e2-7683-5850-8d9c-30d28cd8ea87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c12d1ec-bcf8-590c-8a5a-6919ad803ae9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ae55615-aa0b-56be-abe4-4e3656ffeae1" "answer-6c12d1ec-bcf8-590c-8a5a-6919ad803ae9" "answer-4b48315c-b88d-56e7-9163-6a5e7a7cbe4a" "answer-6e7dd96c-e586-5a90-8e07-97c084f41cb0" "answer-648641f9-e71c-5fc8-9d96-1b43ce6d37d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ae55615-aa0b-56be-abe4-4e3656ffeae1" :answer/id #uuid "9ae55615-aa0b-56be-abe4-4e3656ffeae1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(16k-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c12d1ec-bcf8-590c-8a5a-6919ad803ae9" :answer/id #uuid "6c12d1ec-bcf8-590c-8a5a-6919ad803ae9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b48315c-b88d-56e7-9163-6a5e7a7cbe4a" :answer/id #uuid "4b48315c-b88d-56e7-9163-6a5e7a7cbe4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(4k-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e7dd96c-e586-5a90-8e07-97c084f41cb0" :answer/id #uuid "6e7dd96c-e586-5a90-8e07-97c084f41cb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(16k-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-648641f9-e71c-5fc8-9d96-1b43ce6d37d8" :answer/id #uuid "648641f9-e71c-5fc8-9d96-1b43ce6d37d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-66f132c4-a82c-5cf3-bd20-df4c38d0e1c3" :question/id #uuid "66f132c4-a82c-5cf3-bd20-df4c38d0e1c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-44099" :question/problem "Factor $-9{x}^{2}+12x-4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{x}^{2}+12x-4 & = \\\\ -(9{x}^{2}-12x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-12x+4 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-12x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2\\cdot (6x)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(9{x}^{2}-12x+4) & = \\\\ -(3x-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da20f012-448f-53f8-b542-42b6efc9029d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da20f012-448f-53f8-b542-42b6efc9029d" :answer-field/id #uuid "da20f012-448f-53f8-b542-42b6efc9029d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1a88e3be-86b1-5646-b222-bbff79557817" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a88e3be-86b1-5646-b222-bbff79557817" "answer-f3efad0b-d86f-5a94-8f83-37eb2ff02102" "answer-9c76dc5d-cdf5-5206-9e60-63b938d2aa3f" "answer-a0f78be8-45b5-538b-b88a-dead6b1bcfe4" "answer-f9f449cc-10f0-55f1-9b6f-97ae4ad993b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a88e3be-86b1-5646-b222-bbff79557817" :answer/id #uuid "1a88e3be-86b1-5646-b222-bbff79557817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(3x-2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3efad0b-d86f-5a94-8f83-37eb2ff02102" :answer/id #uuid "f3efad0b-d86f-5a94-8f83-37eb2ff02102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3(x-2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c76dc5d-cdf5-5206-9e60-63b938d2aa3f" :answer/id #uuid "9c76dc5d-cdf5-5206-9e60-63b938d2aa3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3(x-4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0f78be8-45b5-538b-b88a-dead6b1bcfe4" :answer/id #uuid "a0f78be8-45b5-538b-b88a-dead6b1bcfe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9(x+2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9f449cc-10f0-55f1-9b6f-97ae4ad993b8" :answer/id #uuid "f9f449cc-10f0-55f1-9b6f-97ae4ad993b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9(x-2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2d099da-828c-5882-bf9e-a7fe2ace1301" :question/id #uuid "f2d099da-828c-5882-bf9e-a7fe2ace1301" :question/math-academy-id "q-111647" :question/problem "Factor $-4{m}^{2}-4m-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{m}^{2}-4m-1 & = \\\\ -(4{m}^{2}+4m+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{m}^{2}+4m+1 & = \\\\ (2m)^{2}+4m+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2m)^{2}+2\\cdot (2m\\cdot 1)+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2m+1)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(4{m}^{2}+4m+1) & = \\\\ -(2m+1)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0430d0ae-ab49-5bbf-9b16-144277f1be0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0430d0ae-ab49-5bbf-9b16-144277f1be0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "0430d0ae-ab49-5bbf-9b16-144277f1be0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4439d6fb-27f4-5625-aa24-7ff658456c79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2766e46-2765-5512-99cd-6d27f8962334" "answer-974b777e-f890-588c-9588-cccf11336c35" "answer-4439d6fb-27f4-5625-aa24-7ff658456c79" "answer-398e9906-9a7d-594a-9e87-6f270ffa1a92" "answer-51d1d0d6-f508-5bef-a7f1-f1142c1e7206"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2766e46-2765-5512-99cd-6d27f8962334" :answer/id #uuid "c2766e46-2765-5512-99cd-6d27f8962334" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2m+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-974b777e-f890-588c-9588-cccf11336c35" :answer/id #uuid "974b777e-f890-588c-9588-cccf11336c35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4m-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4439d6fb-27f4-5625-aa24-7ff658456c79" :answer/id #uuid "4439d6fb-27f4-5625-aa24-7ff658456c79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(2m+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-398e9906-9a7d-594a-9e87-6f270ffa1a92" :answer/id #uuid "398e9906-9a7d-594a-9e87-6f270ffa1a92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(2m-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51d1d0d6-f508-5bef-a7f1-f1142c1e7206" :answer/id #uuid "51d1d0d6-f508-5bef-a7f1-f1142c1e7206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(4m+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa78e3d6-5bc5-5702-a92b-18645aa6667d" :question/id #uuid "fa78e3d6-5bc5-5702-a92b-18645aa6667d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79011" :question/problem "Factor $-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25} & = \\\\ -(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2x)+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25}) & = \\\\ -{(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57108f3e-71a1-522a-91b8-44b0f9c102f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57108f3e-71a1-522a-91b8-44b0f9c102f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "57108f3e-71a1-522a-91b8-44b0f9c102f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7b14baa-96be-5e29-b3ca-fac7296e27f0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db7b288e-4139-5805-81c0-76fbc3968f9d" "answer-c7b14baa-96be-5e29-b3ca-fac7296e27f0" "answer-ed1478d7-0e89-5af8-bc77-28ddc339e954" "answer-c924f071-a17f-553b-bde2-d655fd8293fa" "answer-c6b62849-07f9-5df6-9e5f-bb5d41036669"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db7b288e-4139-5805-81c0-76fbc3968f9d" :answer/id #uuid "db7b288e-4139-5805-81c0-76fbc3968f9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7b14baa-96be-5e29-b3ca-fac7296e27f0" :answer/id #uuid "c7b14baa-96be-5e29-b3ca-fac7296e27f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed1478d7-0e89-5af8-bc77-28ddc339e954" :answer/id #uuid "ed1478d7-0e89-5af8-bc77-28ddc339e954" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(4x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c924f071-a17f-553b-bde2-d655fd8293fa" :answer/id #uuid "c924f071-a17f-553b-bde2-d655fd8293fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6b62849-07f9-5df6-9e5f-bb5d41036669" :answer/id #uuid "c6b62849-07f9-5df6-9e5f-bb5d41036669" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069940 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1.$ For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4,$ and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2(2x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1,$ we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a+b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+12x+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(6x)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(2x\\cdot 3)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069954 :tutorial/content "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a-b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069941 :tutorial/content "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n\n$$\n-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,?\n$$\n\n \nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $−1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-20y+4 & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-20y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(10y)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(5y\\cdot 2)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(25{y}^{2}-20y+4) & = \\\\ -(5y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068930 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186067732 :answer-field/choices 17592186067735] [:db/retract 17592186067732 :answer-field/choices 17592186067736] [:db/retract 17592186067732 :answer-field/choices 17592186067737] {:db/id 17592186068850 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4588ac42-592a-56c9-ba95-db8993497d1e" "question-95521e10-e107-52c3-9a4a-46859902da66"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068467 :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-8x^6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-8{x}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2{x}^{2})\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-2{x}^{2})^{3}} & = \\\\ -2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068796 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a86cd21a-bd7e-5314-bf81-48804b9affac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068639 :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{(5x - 4)^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The expression underneath the radical is already a perfect cube. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(5x-4)^{3}} & =5x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068851 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fe75fff3-f9bd-5fd1-9943-ab7910546025" "question-aff1f300-1ee4-5b20-aeea-457d6b5a90c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068468 :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{a^3}{(b-1)^3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{a}^{3}}{(b-1)^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}\\cdot \\frac{a}{(b-1)}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{a}{b-1})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{a}{b-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4588ac42-592a-56c9-ba95-db8993497d1e" :question/id #uuid "4588ac42-592a-56c9-ba95-db8993497d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-52309" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{6}{b}^{6}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{6}{b}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{a}^{2}{b}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}{b}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{({a}^{2}{b}^{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ {a}^{2}{b}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-744ae46d-8d69-511a-b7d4-25e7112917fc"] :db/ensure 